{
  // Layout of Black Ops 4's weapon tunables asset.
  //
  // The numeric fields are named by the weapons that survive from an earlier title, where the
  // field names are known: a value carried over unchanged sits at some offset here, and an offset
  // holding the same field's value across many weapons is that field. Every weapon type that
  // title exports is a donor, since they all share the one struct.
  //
  // What is kept:
  //   - three or more adjacent offsets matching three or more fields adjacent in the earlier
  //     struct, in the same order, which is a whole group landing intact;
  //   - two adjacent offsets where at least 60 weapons agree on at least three distinct values and
  //     the name also appears in the field list a debug build of this lineage declares.
  //
  // Competing runs are settled by weight of evidence, longest and best attested first, and a run
  // that would take an offset already spoken for is dropped whole rather than fragmented. Claiming
  // in address order instead silently costs the stronger run its members: the sway group lost
  // three of its six that way, and two of the remaining three landed on worse offsets than the
  // data supports.
  //
  // 'observed' is how many of the loaded assets set the field. It is recomputed from the export
  // rather than written by hand, so a layout can be checked against the game instead of
  // trusted: a field that stops being set is a field that moved.
  //
  // 'agreed', where a field has one, is the older measure - how many of the shared weapons
  // agreed on that field when the name was placed. It is how well attested the name is, which
  // is a different question from how many assets carry a value, and worth keeping apart.
  //
  // 219 weapons are present in both titles.
  //
  // Offsets are only valid for the struct size recorded here.
  //
  // Audited afterwards, which is what settles whether a name sits on its own field or on a
  // neighbour that happens to carry similar numbers. For every offset the audit asks which
  // field fits it best, and for every field which offset fits it best. Being contradicted is
  // not itself wrong - a title re-tunes a weapon and the value legitimately differs - but
  // being contradicted more often than agreed while a distant offset carries the field better
  // is the mark of a misplaced name, and that is how a contiguous but poorly supported run
  // beats a scattered but well supported one.
  //
  // Fields failing that test were moved where the better offset was unclaimed and the two
  // chose each other, and dropped otherwise. A field flagged only because an adjacent sibling
  // fits marginally better is left alone: fields holding the same value as one another cannot
  // be separated by value at all, and their order within the run is what tells them apart.

  // Eight fields here were not matched that way at all, and are held to a higher standard than
  // the rest. The game reports a weapon's own values for them, so the value is known before the
  // offset is looked for: an offset qualifies only when it carries the reported value on every
  // weapon asked, and when its column varies as much as the reported values do. That second
  // condition is what rules out a column of zeroes, which otherwise fits any field whose values
  // round to zero. Each of the eight is explained by exactly one offset, so nothing was chosen
  // between candidates.
  //
  // Two of them are stored as whole milliseconds where the game reports seconds, which is why
  // looking for the reported value as a float finds nothing.

  "struct": "WeaponTunables",
  "pool": 22,
  "size": 4632,
  "fields": [
    // How much a shot is scaled by where it lands, which is the difference between a headshot
    // mattering and not. The struct only points at the nineteen values; they live wherever the
    // pointer leads. Entry 0 is the default and the rest run head downwards in the pairs a body
    // comes in - a sniper rifle reads 1, 2.4, 2.4, 1.5, 1.5 then ones, an assault rifle 1, 1.12,
    // 1.12, and a knife scales nothing at all.
    //
    // The neighbouring title carries the same table with the same nineteen entries, one of them
    // value for value: 1, 4, 4, 5, 1, 1, 0.9 then 0.8 0.8, 0.6 0.6, 0.4 0.4 twice over. That the
    // two agree is what says the length is right, and the twentieth word reads zero on 1,026 of
    // the 1,045 that can be read.
    //
    // The game's code reads 21 of them, though: its hit location function (0x7ff7916e16c0 in
    // BlackOps4_dump.exe) indexes this table by hit location, and the exe's hit location names
    // (rdata 0x7ff7923d6170) are 21 - none, helmet, head, neck, torso_upper, torso_mid,
    // torso_lower, right/left_arm_upper, right/left_arm_lower, right/left_hand, right/left_leg_upper,
    // right/left_leg_lower, right/left_foot, gun, riotshield (Rifling games/bo4.md). The twentieth,
    // the gun, is the zero above; the twenty-first the riot shield.
    //
    // The two pointers after this one lead to tables every weapon shares rather than its own, so
    // they are not this and are left unnamed.
    {"offset": "0x0", "name": "locationDamageMultipliers", "source": "engine", "kind": "float", "form": "float[]@", "count": 21, "maximum": 100, "observed": 1093, "agreed": 1045, "examples": []},

    // What a shot keeps of its speed when it hits a surface, along it and into it, one value per
    // surface. The two are declared side by side in a debug build of this lineage and sit side by
    // side here too: the second table begins exactly 160 bytes after the first, which is what says
    // each holds forty.
    //
    // The variety is all in what bounces. 442 of the 445 bullet weapons and every melee weapon
    // leave both at the default half; grenades carry the spread, and a crossbow bolt reads zero
    // for both because it sticks. A frag grenade keeps 0.7 of its speed along a surface and 0.2
    // into it, which is a grenade skidding rather than rebounding.
    {"offset": "0x8", "name": "parallelBounce", "source": "engine", "kind": "float", "form": "float[]@", "count": 40, "maximum": 100, "observed": 1093, "examples": []},

    {"offset": "0x10", "name": "perpendicularBounce", "source": "engine", "kind": "float", "form": "float[]@", "count": 40, "maximum": 100, "observed": 1093, "examples": []},

