    // Damage is a staircase rather than a number: six damages against the six ranges they reach
    // to, read together because neither array means anything without the other. A weapon that
    // steps down once uses the first and last stop and leaves the four between them zero, so the
    // zeroes are written out - dropping them would move every stop after them.
    //
    // The two stops the game exposes to script are the ends of the range array: what it calls
    // maxdamagerange is damageRange[0] and mindamagerange is damageRange[5]. They were named as
    // separate fields here before the array they belong to was recognised.
    //
    // The same weapon is a different asset in each mode, and the curves differ accordingly:
    // ar_accurate_t8 reads 34 -> 27 in multiplayer, 32 -> 27 -> 24 in blackout, 120 -> 100 in
    // zombies and 200 -> 140 upgraded.
    //
    // The curve ends at the last damage that is set. Past that the ranges carry a default pair,
    // 15000 out to 16000, which is the engine saying the shot does not fall off at all rather than
    // a stop the damage forgot to fill - 184 assets read that way and none of them is wrong.
    //
    // Eleven assets step up rather than down, and the game reports the same numbers for them, so
    // they are read right. Seven are weapons a falloff curve does not describe: thrown, projectile,
    // turret and scorestreak weapons, four of which carry the no-falloff ranges above. The other
    // four are zombies assault rifles whose middle stops genuinely dip.
    {"offset": "0xbec", "name": "damage", "source": "engine", "kind": "int", "form": "int[]", "count": 6, "observed": 861, "agreed": 1092, "examples": []},
    {"offset": "0xc08", "name": "deployablemaxheight", "source": "engine", "kind": "int", "form": "int", "observed": 1092, "examples": []},
    {"offset": "0xc10", "name": "deployableminheight", "source": "engine", "kind": "int", "form": "int", "observed": 1091, "examples": []},

